{"page":771,"blocks":[{"x":0.62471,"y":0.05257,"w":0.32681,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"13.1 Curves in Space and Their Tangents 757"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.09345,"w":0.59302,"h":0.05883,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"The last of these equalities holds because the limit of the cross product of two vector func- tions is the cross product of their limits if the latter exist (Exercise 32). As h approaches zero, v(t + h) approaches v(t) because v, being differentiable at t, is continuous at t (Exer- cise 33). The two fractions approach the values of du>dt and dv>dt at t. In short,"},{"x":0.54524,"y":0.15913,"w":0.11105,"h":0.02728,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"d dt (u * v) = du"},{"x":0.64133,"y":0.15913,"w":0.12182,"h":0.02728,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"dt * v + u * dv"},{"x":0.74864,"y":0.16635,"w":0.02031,"h":0.02008,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt ."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.2036,"w":0.59303,"h":0.05995,"fs":0.01311,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Proof of the Chain Rule Suppose that u(s) = a(s)i + b(s)j + c(s)k is a differen- tiable vector function of s and that s = ƒ(t) is a differentiable scalar function of t. Then a, b, and c are differentiable functions of t, and the Chain Rule for differentiable real-valued functions gives"},{"x":0.51889,"y":0.26589,"w":0.10011,"h":0.0305,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"d dt 3u(s)4 = da"},{"x":0.60449,"y":0.26912,"w":0.06196,"h":0.02727,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt i + db"},{"x":0.66652,"y":0.26912,"w":0.0495,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"j + dc"},{"x":0.70195,"y":0.27631,"w":0.0253,"h":0.02008,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt k"},{"x":0.65194,"y":0.2836,"w":0.01271,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt"},{"x":0.09716,"y":0.28888,"w":0.23576,"h":0.06777,"fs":0.01151,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"As an algebraic convenience, we sometimes write the product of a scalar c and a vector v as vc instead of cv. This permits us, for instance, to write the Chain Rule in a familiar form:"},{"x":0.58506,"y":0.29981,"w":0.03342,"h":0.01999